================================================================================================================================
0. the estimator, validated against the two analytic limits BEFORE any data is touched
================================================================================================================================
  isolated deep-MOND check: sigma(estimator)/sigma((4/81 G M a0)^1/4) = 1.0425 +- 0.0110 over 6 (M_b, r_h) pairs
  [PASS] V1 estimator validation -- in the isolated deep-MOND limit the Wolf-style estimator sigma^2 = a(r_h) r_h/3 reproduces the exact MOND relation sigma^4 = (4/81) G M_b a_0 to better than 10%   (ratio 1.0425 +- 0.0110)
  EFE-dominated limit: G_eff/[nu(y_e) G] = 0.514 - 0.575 over y_e = 0.003-0.1 (deep-MOND value 1/2)
  [PASS] V2 estimator validation -- the correct EFE-dominated effective G is nu(y_e)(1+dln nu/dln y) ~ nu(y_e)/2, i.e. the naive G_eff = nu(y_ext) G over-predicts the EFE-suppressed acceleration by a factor 2.  This is the h8 warning, now derived   (ratio range 0.514-0.575, deep-MOND asymptote 0.500)

================================================================================================================================
1. the samples
================================================================================================================================
  LVD Milky Way satellites with a measured (not upper-limit) dispersion: 45  ->  31 ULTRA-FAINT (M_V > -7.7) + 14 classical;   M_V range -18.1 to -1.1
  LVD M31 satellites with a measured dispersion: 34;   Collins+2013 (item 44's named source): 14 of 18 rows (4 carry sigV = 0.0, i.e. an unresolved dispersion, and are excluded as upper limits)
  D_host range: MW 12-372 kpc; M31(LVD) 6-1183 kpc; M31(Collins, 3-D from RA/Dec/D) 72-476 kpc
  LVD isolated local-field dwarfs with a measured dispersion: 13.  Their combined MW+M31 external Newtonian field is 0.26%-15.04% of their own internal one (median 1.88%), so they are an EFE-FREE control: any failure there is a failure of the isolated law, not of the external-field term.

