  canonical  LUMINOSITY-MATCHED (-8 >= M_V >= -13) centring Upsilon_V:  MW (N=10)  16.2 (scatter 0.160) | M31 LVD (N=20)  13.6 (0.174) | M31 Collins (N=9)  19.8 (0.190)
  alt        LUMINOSITY-MATCHED (-8 >= M_V >= -13) centring Upsilon_V:  MW (N=10)  14.0 (scatter 0.161) | M31 LVD (N=20)  12.5 (0.175) | M31 Collins (N=9)  17.6 (0.189)
  the raw MW-vs-M31 gap seen above is therefore MOSTLY a census effect, not a host effect: at matched luminosity the two
  satellite systems ask for similar mass-to-light ratios.  Reported this way round because the un-matched comparison would
  have looked like a much sharper kill than it is.
  [PASS] 44c AGAINST INTEREST, in the direction that WEAKENS the apparent kill: the framework demands ONE Upsilon_V for every old stellar population in the Local Group, and the raw MW and M31 samples appear to demand very different ones -- but once the two samples are matched in luminosity the gap largely closes.  The surviving statement is the weaker (and true) one: BOTH systems need a mass-to-light ratio of order 10, several times any stellar population, with ~0.25 dex of irreducible scatter   (canonical: matched MW 16.2 vs M31 13.6 (ratio 1.19x), unmatched ratio was 2.7x; alt: matched MW 14.0 vs M31 12.5 (ratio 1.12x), unmatched ratio was 2.7x)

================================================================================================================================
4. the EFE-FREE control: the isolated Local-Group dwarfs
================================================================================================================================
  canonical  isolated field dwarfs (N=13): at Ups_V=2, median -0.044 dex, scatter 0.124 dex; centring Ups_V = 1.3 (scatter 0.128 dex).   gas-poor subset (N=5: Antlia B, Cetus, Phoenix, Tucana, UGC 4879): at Ups_V=2 median +0.059, scatter 0.080 dex; centring Ups_V = 3.3 (scatter 0.083 dex)
  alt        isolated field dwarfs (N=13): at Ups_V=2, median -0.062 dex, scatter 0.124 dex; centring Ups_V = 1.1 (scatter 0.130 dex).   gas-poor subset (N=5: Antlia B, Cetus, Phoenix, Tucana, UGC 4879): at Ups_V=2 median +0.040, scatter 0.080 dex; centring Ups_V = 2.9 (scatter 0.082 dex)
  caveat both ways: the gas-rich members of this control (NGC 6822, WLM, IC 1613, Leo A, Pegasus, Sag dIrr) are ROTATING, so
  their line-of-sight sigma understates the support and the framework is being handed an easy target there; the gas-poor
  subset is the honest one and it is only a handful of galaxies.
  [PASS] 43e the EFE-free control localises the failure: the ISOLATED Local-Group dwarfs, where the external-field term is switched off entirely, sit far closer to the framework's isolated law than the satellites do.  So the ultra-faint liability is NOT a failure of a_0 or of the kernel -- it is concentrated in satellites, where tides, binaries and the EFE all act at once   (canonical: isolated field centring Upsilon_V = 1.3 (N=13, scatter 0.128 dex) and gas-poor 3.3 (N=5), against 62.9 for the MW satellites and 109.0 for the ultra-faints)
  [PASS] 43f the one place a Kepler-grade scatter DOES appear is the gas-poor isolated dwarfs -- and it is UNDERPOWERED, not a hit: five galaxies with one free Upsilon_V is 4 degrees of freedom, so a sub-0.1-dex scatter there is not evidence of anything.  Recorded so that nobody quotes it as a second law.  What it IS good for is the contrast with the satellites, which is large   (canonical gas-poor isolated: scatter 0.083 dex on N=5 at centring Upsilon_V = 3.3; a 0.1-dex bar needs N of order 30 before it means anything, and the whole isolated Local Group offers 13)

