6. mutation controls
================================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation -- shuffling the measured dispersions between satellites must inflate the scatter of the law   (shuffled 0.612 vs real 0.331 dex)
  [PASS] M2 mutation -- a_0 inflated 10x must move the law's zero point by ~0.25 dex (sigma ~ a_0^{1/4} in the deep-MOND limit)   (median shifts +0.001 -> -0.220 dex (+0.222))
  [PASS] M3 mutation -- switching the kernel off (nu = 1, pure Newton with the same baryons) must make the fit worse, not better   (Newtonian median offset +0.299 dex vs framework +0.001 dex at the same Upsilon_V)

================================================================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================================================================
  ITEM 43 -- NOT a second law, and a LIABILITY.  The ultra-faint dwarfs do not lie on a sigma(M_b, D_host) line with a single
  a_0 and a stellar-population mass-to-light ratio: at Upsilon_V = 2 they sit 0.83 dex above the framework's external-field
  prediction, centring needs Upsilon_V ~ 109, and even after Upsilon_V is tuned freely the residual scatter is 0.23 dex --
  two to three times the 0.1-dex Kepler-grade bar.  This is the known MOND ultra-faint problem, reproduced on a larger and newer
  sample (Local Volume Database) with the correct QUMOND external-field formula, not a new discovery.  LambdaCDM absorbs the
  same data with one free halo per dwarf (the Wolf M/L_V runs 62-2489).  A SECOND negative found here, and it is the weaker of the two: the
  residual correlates with the host distance at 2.0-3.0 sigma after controlling for luminosity (3.0 in least squares on all 45,
  2.0-2.5 in the rank version and on the ultra-faints alone; sign and slope robust to every cut in section 2b), so the
  external-field term itself is
  mis-shaped on the Milky Way's satellites, not merely mis-normalised.
  ITEM 44 -- also NOT Kepler-grade: Collins+2013's M31 dSphs need Upsilon_V ~ 24 with 0.25 dex of scatter.  My first
  framing of this -- 'the MW and M31 demand different mass-to-light ratios, which a one-constant law cannot survive' -- did NOT
  hold up: matched in luminosity the two systems agree to 20%, and the apparent gap was a census effect.  The surviving
  statement is the weaker and true one, that BOTH satellite systems need a mass-to-light ratio of order 10-20 and carry 0.25 dex
  of irreducible scatter.  Booked as a LIABILITY on both footings, but a shared Local-Group one rather than a host-dependent one.
  THE ONE RESULT THAT CUTS THE OTHER WAY, and it is the most interesting thing in the run: switch the external field OFF and the
  same estimator on the same catalogue lands on the ISOLATED Local-Group dwarfs at Upsilon_V = 1.3 (gas-poor subset 3.3) with
  0.13 dex of scatter.  The satellite liability is therefore localised in satellites -- tides, binaries and the EFE -- and is
  NOT a failure of a_0 or of the Route A kernel.  That subsample is only 13 galaxies (5 gas-poor) and is recorded as
  UNDERPOWERED, not as a hit; it is a target for a future pass, not a claim.
  ITEM 8 CORRECTION -- h8's external-field prescription (nu evaluated at the TRUE field rather than the Newtonian one, with no
  (1+dln nu/dln y) factor) is not the QUMOND one, and the factor-2 warning h8 recorded is real -- but only in the EFE-DOMINATED
  limit, which Local Group satellites are not in.  On this data the two recipes differ by 0.05 dex, so item 8's liability is not
  an artefact of its recipe.  It stands.
  Caveats stated both ways: the ultra-faint dispersions are themselves contested (binaries, tidal heating, N_star ~ 10-100),
  the point-mass approximation for the host field is marginal for the innermost satellites, eq. 60 is a one-dimensional EFE
  formula that neglects the direction of the external field, and the gas-rich isolated dwarfs rotate, so their line-of-sight
  dispersion understates their support and flatters the framework.

